---
import Example from './Example.astro';
---
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="tending-title">
  <div class="joint signal" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pulse"></span></div>
  <p class="label kicker">// self-tending</p>
  <h2 id="tending-title">It watches its own reads, and asks before it adds an index</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The engine records the shape and duration of every read. An advisor turns shapes that are
    frequent and slow into <code>_system/IndexRecommendation</code> rows, and builds nothing
    itself. An agent holding the <code>substrate.index</code> grant approves one with
    <code>propose_index</code>. It takes effect on the call, the index builds in the background,
    and an audit event records why. That
    works on proposed types, like <code>ops/Event</code> here; on a type defined in code, the
    recommendation is advice for its author.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    This run forces it, on Postgres. The steps marked “Demo” seed events straight into the store
    and slow every read of <code>ops</code> by 60 ms, so the advisor recommends within one run
    instead of after real traffic; a served binary's advisor reads every ten minutes. The last read
    returns the same rows, and the example's test runs <code>EXPLAIN</code> on the statement the
    engine sent and checks that the plan uses the new index.
  </p>
  <div class="run">
    <Example name="self-tending" show={['namespace.ts']} />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .run {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
</style>
